From dc4fd00d3ea22eaf4a48db4dc4412bcc766bb3a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakshman Turlapati <lakshmanturlapati@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 07:27:46 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(22-05): drive the extension enable toggle only from
 settled WebUI state

The full-lifecycle smoke controller stalled at kReloaded: the real
chrome.management.setEnabled() DOM click on the item card's #enableToggle
reported "clicked" but no matching ExtensionRegistryObserver unload ever
arrived. Root-caused by direct observation, not inference.

ClickDisableToggle() ran synchronously from inside the browser-side
ExtensionRegistryObserver callback for the preceding transition, 4.5 ms after
the reload's native OnExtensionLoaded. chrome://extensions learns about a
registry transition through its own independent
developerPrivate.onItemStateChanged round trip, so the card still rendered the
*previous* state. An instrumented run captured exactly that at the first
disable click:

  {"expect":true,"checked_before":false,"hidden":false,"disabled":false,
   "state":"DISABLED","reasons":{... "reloading":true ...},"mgmt":"function"}

A stale render is neither hidden nor disabled -- userCanChangeEnablement()
ignores disableReasons.reloading and getEnableControl() still returns
ENABLE_TOGGLE -- so the existing pre-checks passed and the click landed on a
toggle whose checked was false. cr-toggle flipped it to true and
extensions-item::onEnableToggleChange_() called
chrome.management.setEnabled(id, /*enabled=*/true) on an already-enabled
extension. ManagementSetEnabledFunction takes neither branch in that case,
succeeds, and mutates nothing. The click genuinely happened; it just requested
the state the extension was already in. Two things hid it:
cr_toggle.ts::toggleState_() fires `change` only after `await
this.updateComplete`, so the injected script's "clicked" return value is
emitted before the API call is made, and service.ts::setItemEnabled() ends in
`.catch(_ => {})`.

- EnableToggleClickScript() now takes expect_checked and returns a new
  retryable `toggle_state_not_settled` when the rendered checked value
  disagrees, instead of clicking a stale toggle. Disable passes true, enable
  passes false; both click-issued handlers fold the new status into their
  existing bounded 10s retry. The retry 204 ms later saw state:"ENABLED",
  checked:true, and the matching OnExtensionUnloaded (reason 1) arrived 0.9 ms
  after the click. The enable stage reproduced the exact mirror image, so the
  mechanism is direction-symmetric rather than a one-off.
- Same root cause, newly reachable stage: ExercisePolicyGrants() no longer runs
  inside OnExtensionLoaded. ExtensionRegistrar::ActivateExtension() calls
  ExtensionRegistry::AddReady()/TriggerOnReady() only *after* the
  TriggerOnLoaded() observer loop, and every PrometheusExtensionPolicyService
  decision is gated on ready_extensions().Contains(id) -- a not-yet-ready
  extension is inactive, so grant mutations are refused with
  kInactiveExtension (the policy_read_only_mutation_denied hit next) and
  GetEffectiveGrant() clamps to Blocked. New bounded PollExtensionReady() waits
  for ExtensionSystem::is_ready() plus real ready-set membership first.
- Retire the satisfied wait's pending_wait_generation_ at each transition, so a
  completed stage's still-queued timeout cannot fail the smoke under the
  previous stage's reason across the new retry loop.
- Raise the browser test's assertion boundary kReloaded -> kEnabled, which is
  what this commit actually verifies.

No verification path weakened: no MV2 enablement, no install-verification
bypass, no accept_dialog_for_testing_, no direct installer/registrar mutation.
The verify script's static no-bypass guards pass.

Verified: verify-phase-22-extension-smoke.sh --focused exits 0 (32 tests, zero
retry, skip, or failure). 13 extra direct runs of
FullLifecycleInstallsInspectsAndReloads: 11 pass; both failures are the
pre-existing environment flakes already documented in 22-05-SUMMARY, each
outside the test body (macOS WasLaunchedAsLoginItemRestoreState() BUS_ADRALN at
startup, and the pre-existing DCHECK on PostMainMessageLoopRun()'s first line
at shutdown). No run failed with "never reached kEnabled".

Deliberately still not asserted past kEnabled, because two separate defects sit
in the options/remove stretch and would fail this test on their own account
rather than on this plan's contract: a renderer terminated for the bad Mojo
message "Tried to manipulate an event listener for an unauthorized extension
ID." shortly after the rapid disable/enable churn plus options-tab open, and a
remove click that reports success and accepts the native confirmation dialog
without any OnExtensionUninstalled arriving. Both are under active
investigation in the same debug session; Plan 22-05 Tasks 3 and 4 remain
unstarted.
